  "text": "**Solution 1 is correct: the ratio is r.** I could not check this against the literature in this session, so the verdict rests on a plausibility argument and the recalled form of the result.\n\n- **Solution 2 ($q^{2D(r-1)}$)** rests on counting cosets of a Borel-type subgroup, which is not the right invariant. Its own reasoning admits the diagonal contribution is uncertain.\n- **Solution 3 ($q^{D(r-1)}$)** rests on the claim that wild diamond operators act unipotently and inflate each generalized eigenspace by $|W_r|$. That argument never shows why the eigenspace dimension should scale by the group order, and the Hecke algebra does not force that. It also conflates the finite-slope dimension growth with the multiplicity of a fixed eigensystem.\n- **Solution 1 ($r$)** matches the linear pattern expected from the old-form tower $f(\\wp^j z)$ for $0 \\le j \\le r-1$. Its multiplicity grows linearly in the level exponent, which is consistent with control-theorem results of the Gouvêa–Mazur/Buzzard type, where finite-slope dimensions grow linearly in the level exponent. It also gives 1 at $r=1$, as required.\n\nSolution 1's justification is only a sketch, but its answer is the most plausible of the three.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
